dding into the system  and we also don't want released the grid  structure however we do know how the  public and  quantities are related to each other  they're related through a problem called  AC optimal power flow and this is  basically the mechanism through which  grid operators schedule electricity  generation in order to minimize the  system costs and so the way we formulate  our reverse engineering problem the  ability to reverse engineer private data  from public is there something we are  calling inverse optimal power flow  essentially can we find the inputs to  the AC opf problem to the AC optimal  power flow problem that produce public  outputs that look like the data that we  actually observe on the system so  basically if we estimate generator costs  and grid structure do we actually get  power generation and electricity prices  that match the powered power generation  at electricity prices that we see in the  real world so the way we solve this  inverse optimal power flow problem is  through gradient descent a pretty  standard method and here's the  pseudocode for that algorithm at a high  level what the pseudocode is saying is  that we start with a bunch of public  data and also some initial guesses for  what we think that the private parameter  is what we think the costs and grid  structure should be we then going  through this basically we take our  guesses we put it through the AC optimal  powerful algorithm and we get some  estimates for what we think the public  quantities should be and we compute the  loss between or the error between the  public quantities that we estimate and  the public quantities that we actually  see and then we use the information  about that error to update our estimates  for what we think the private data is so  essentially while there is some gap  between what our algorithm thinks the  public data should be and what the  public data actually is we iteratively  use the gradient of this error to update  our parameters and when our outputs  match the real public data we stop so in  principle this algorithm looks actually  relatively simple but there's something  tricky about it that I haven't told you  yet and that is exactly what does this  ace  optimal power flow problem look like and  in particular AC optimal power flow is  actually an optimization problem so the  formulation is here on the slide and in  particular grid operators will try to  minimize electricity costs that's the  objective of the problem subject to some  linear inequality and equality  constraints that we might have on the  system as well as one can straight than  we call the power flow constraint that  basically describes the physical  attributes of the power grid to relate  this to the inverse problem that I show  above this variable Z that power  operators are optimizing over is what  contains the power generation and for  those who this means anything to the  dual variables on that power flow  constraint are actually what our  electricity prices are but for those who  may not know what that means basically  the AC optimal power flow formulation is  what is giving us our estimates of power  generation and electricity prices and  the tricky part about this is that now  what you see on the screen in front of  you is an optimization problem embedded  within an optimization problem and this  is actually what we're trying to somehow  take gradients through and so to go back  to our stochastic gradient descent or a  gradient descent algorithm here to  compute that particular gradient that's  highlighted in the red box to compute  the gradient that we need to update our  public parameter guesses we need to  compute something like this so in  particular for each parameter we're  trying to guess we have to compute the  gradient of the loss with respect to  that parameter and if you run out the  chain rule you'll get something that  looks like this and in particular the  thing I'd like to highlight is that  taking this 